# The build context is the repository root: docker/docker-compose.yml builds with `context: ..` so
# the Dockerfile can COPY docker/entrypoint.sh and scripts/write-confs.sh out of the checkout. The
# image needs nothing else, and without this file the daemon would send the whole tree - docs,
# modules, patches, SQL, a local build - on every build.
#
# Everything is excluded by name rather than with a leading `*`: the exceptions below are the only
# files the build reads, and naming the exclusions keeps a new top-level directory from silently
# becoming part of the context.

.git
.github
build
docs
modules
patches
sql
pins.toml
README.md
LICENSE

# scripts/ except the four files the image copies: write-confs.sh (the realm's conf writer, shared
# with scripts/thor-install.sh) and the client-data patcher with the generator and the MPQ library
# it loads. Every one of them is named, because a `!dir/` re-includes the directory and not
# what is under it - `scripts/**` above still matches the file, and the COPY fails at build time with
# "not found" rather than in any way the installer reports.
scripts/**
!scripts/write-confs.sh
!scripts/item-dbc/patch_item_dbc.py
!scripts/loot-bags/gen_loot_bags.py
!scripts/lib/dbc_mpq.py

# Local artifacts and noise: the tarballs and client data the docs tell you to download, plus
# anything a build or an editor leaves behind.
*.log
*.zst
*.zip
*.tar
*.sha256

# Secrets that sit beside the tree when the docs are followed from here: -Root/--root stage, the
# compose .env, and the credentials file both installers write. The daemon is often remote
# (DOCKER_HOST), so anything left in the context leaves the machine.
.env
state.env
credentials.txt
.thor-state
.win-state
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
*.swo
.idea/
.vscode/
